Post-checkpoint clarification: what the parallel optimization work improved

This note records follow-up findings after this checkpoint. The three patches
described below are currently uncommitted working-tree changes; attaching this
note does not imply that their implementation is contained in this commit.

We removed real duplicated work, but did not fix the main latency problem.

1. State handling: private BlueprintStateMachine fork/adopt no longer needlessly
   copies, serializes, parses and hashes already-owned validated state. Public
   snapshots still copy bytes. New operations, reducer execution, authorization
   and validation remain active; no replay-result cache was introduced.
2. Recovery verification: unchanged exact immutable evidence is verified fewer
   times. The matched final seal performed 20 source verifications instead of
   32. Fresh native storage, authority, protection and currency checks remain,
   with full proof charges preserved.
3. Grid rendering: transient movement no longer rebuilds durable-history
   diagnostics or recreates the entire board. This improves a separate UI path;
   the native rollover benchmark does not exercise that rendering.

These are implemented, test-backed reductions in duplication, not merely
cosmetic architectural changes. Verification passed 62 focused tests, source
and Grid type checks, five healthy native rollovers with settlement/cleanup/
persisted reopen/continuation, an unprofiled confirmation, and the actual Grid
movement/rollover browser regression.

However, no meaningful aggregate latency or CPU improvement was demonstrated.
The profiled owned-browser CPU was 141.81 seconds versus 142.51 previously;
the unprofiled run used 128.96 seconds versus 124.42 previously. Unprofiled final
sealing still took 8.67 seconds and creator adoption 2.30 seconds. Host load
qualifies wall-time comparisons but does not establish an environmental cause
or excuse the intrinsic computation. Remaining canonical/context processing,
allocation and native servicing are distributed costs; profiles do not yet
provide an exact every-cycle explanation or establish one dominant function.

Plain English: we removed some unnecessary trips, but the expensive journey is
still there. This batch delivered cleaner ownership, specific computational
reductions and correctness evidence, not the requested gameplay speedup. Do not
count it as latency, endurance, Phase 6/7 or FPS closure. No new endurance run
was started and no resource ceiling or safety requirement was weakened.

